    "text": "DNS Working > We type a website like https://mypage.com in our browser, our computer tries to find the IP address. > First, it checks local cache (our browser, OS, or router) to see if 1t already knows the IP address. > If local cache doesn’t have IP, check host files (used for specific manual mappings). > If still not found, the query is sent to a DNS resolver (usually provided by your ISP). > DNS resolver sends query to a Root DNS server, which points to Top-level domain (TLD) server (.com server for this example). > TLD server then directs the resolver to the authoritative name server for mypage.com . > Authoritative nameserver knows the exact IP address for mypage.com and sends it back to the resolver. This server holds the actual DNS records and responds with the IP address. > Resolver passes the IP address to our computer. > Our computer uses the IP address to connect to the real server where the website is hosted. > The website loads in our browser.",
